  Why you were getting 404 — root cause found

  The relay is not the problem. It's a path-agnostic proxy: a single catch-all route (/<path:path> in archie-service-relay/src/api/routes/relay.py) forwards any path to the mediator over the WebSocket tunnel. No allowlist, no per-route config — it needs zero changes to forward new mediator endpoints.

  The 404 came from the mediator itself. In archie-client-mediator/main.py:
  - Relay-forwarded traffic is dispatched through an internal Flask app (_create_internal_app → _register_business_blueprints(internal_app)), invoked via WSRouter.
  - kill-pod lives in utils_bp, which was registered only on the main HTTP app (the old line 121) and not inside _register_business_blueprints.
  - So kill-pod existed for direct HTTP but was invisible to the tunnel → the internal app had no matching route → mediator returned its own 404 "URL path not found".

  That function's own docstring says "Add new blueprints here — they will automatically work through both HTTP and WebSocket" — utils_bp had just never been added.

  Fixes applied (on branch fix/expose-utils-bp-kill-pod-over-relay, off main)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      

  1. archie-client-mediator/main.py — the real, committable fix:
  - Added utils_bp to _register_business_blueprints (now on both the main app and the WS-dispatch app).
  - Removed the redundant standalone app.register_blueprint(utils_bp, …).

  Verified by replicating main.py's registration: the WS-dispatch app now exposes POST /api/v1/kill-pod (→ 202 with {"confirm": true}, 400 without) and GET /api/v1/runners, with no duplicate-registration error.

  2. src/api/models.py — local-only, not a code bug: Importing src.api was failing because ListRunnersResponse (used by the new list-runners endpoint, #218) was undefined. But models.py is git-ignored and generated from swagger.yaml via make pre-setup. The spec already defines ListRunnersResponse — your local copy was just stale after the pull. I regenerated it (generate-pydantic-models -i swagger.yaml …); CI does this
  automatically, so deployed images were never broken.

  On your two endpoints

  - POST /api/v1/kill-pod — path was correct all along ({"confirm": true} body, X-Client-ID header). Works via relay once the main.py fix is deployed.
  - "list worker pods" = GET /api/v1/runners (list_runners) — lives in runners_bp, already in _register_business_blueprints, so it works over the relay once deployed + models.py regenerated. No code change needed for it.

